# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MICA · C6/W5 — THE DAILY WITNESS REPORT.
#
#   Install path: /etc/systemd/system/mica-report.service
#   Program:      /opt/mica-machine/src/report/mica-report.ts
#   Cadence:      mica-report.timer — NOT this file. See the contrast below.
#
# ── S110-B RECONCILIATION, 2026-09-04 — found stale while wiring this build's
#    own rider, not part of the brief's own named findings ──────────────────
#
#   This dev-tree copy's `ExecStart` had drifted from the live unit BEFORE
#   tonight, back to S108/S109: the live unit already carried
#   `--cadence-file` (S108 DC-2) and `--clerk-stamps-file` (S109 item 3),
#   neither ever folded back here. Not named in the S110 deployment-findings
#   brief — found independently while reconciling this file for its own
#   S110-B rider, and fixed in the same pass rather than left
#   half-reconciled. RULED HOUSE PRACTICE applies here too: unit installs go
#   through this file, `install`ed onto the live path, from now on.
#
#   `User=`/`Group=` are UNCHANGED and MUST STAY THE PLACEHOLDER — R-V3
#   (`witness-report.test.ts`) asserts this file keeps
#   `REPLACE-WITH-THE-REPORT-PRINCIPAL` verbatim; the real principal is a
#   deploy-time declaration, substituted at install, never checked in. This
#   build's first attempt at this reconciliation got that wrong and filled in
#   `mica-report` — the test caught it immediately, before this file was
#   ever written to disk in that state as a commit. Left here as the record
#   of the catch, not as a warning that needs repeating.
# ── THIS IS A ONESHOT, AND `mica-witnessd.service` IS THE CONTRAST ───────────
#
#   The witness daemon is a PROCESS THAT STAYS: it holds a sweep timer, it must
#   be up for the intake to be consumed at all, and its only real decision is
#   `Restart=no` — because a witness that cannot append must not run on looking
#   alive. Everything about that unit is about a thing being CONTINUOUSLY TRUE.
#
#   This unit is the opposite shape. The program opens a file, folds it, writes
#   one report and exits. There is nothing for it to be "up" for; between runs
#   there is deliberately no process at all. So:
#
#     Type=oneshot     it finishes. `simple` would have systemd believe a
#                      finished reader is a crashed daemon.
#     no Restart=      there is nothing to restart. A failed run failed for a
#                      reason the exit code names, and tomorrow's timer will run
#                      the next one regardless.
#     no [Install]     THIS UNIT IS NOT ENABLED. The timer is enabled; the timer
#                      starts this. `systemctl enable mica-report.service` would
#                      instead run it once at boot, which is not a schedule.
#
# ── AND IT IS NOT A MONITOR — the unit must not smuggle in what the code refuses
#
#   There is deliberately NO `After=mica-witnessd.service` and no `Requires=`.
#   This program reads a FILE. Ordering it behind the witness would make the
#   report's existence depend on the witness being up, which is the liveness
#   claim `mica-report.ts` refuses to make — moved out of the code and into the
#   unit, where it would be harder to see. A report whose absence means "the
#   witness was down" is a monitor. This one reports the age of the last record
#   and stops there.
#
# ── S110-B: --registrar-log, the reconciliation reader's Pair A source ──────
#
#   `/var/lib/mica/precommit/registrar-log-chained.jsonl` (`registrar.ts`'s
#   own `chainedLogPath()`) — a DIFFERENT file from everything else this unit
#   already reads, owned by `mica-registrar`. `[TG]` — THIS PRINCIPAL'S READ
#   ACCESS TO IT IS NOT VERIFIED FROM THIS SEAT: `mica-report` is a
#   `mica-readers` member (line above), which is what already gates
#   `mica-log.jsonl` (0640 mica-registrar:mica-readers); whether
#   `registrar-log-chained.jsonl` carries the SAME group is a deployment fact,
#   not read here. If it does not, Pair A reads CANNOT_ASSESS, loud and
#   named, never a silent wrong count — but confirm at bytes before trusting
#   a clean read. No `ReadOnlyPaths=` rider is needed below:
#   `ProtectSystem=strict` restricts WRITES outside `ReadWritePaths=`, not
#   reads, and `/var/lib/mica/precommit` is not under `ProtectHome=`'s scope.
#
# ── S111 (generalized night of the same session): --journal-declarations,
#    the reconciliation reader's pair-FAMILY source — NOT YET ADDED BELOW,
#    DELIBERATELY ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   RULED (the arbiter, S111 night): "generalize — it is just another book." The
#   doctor-only Pair C (`assessDoctorJournalReconciliation`, gated to
#   `mica-doctor` and a single `--doctor-uid` flag) is SUPERSEDED by a
#   per-principal FAMILY (`assessDeclaredJournalReconciliations`): one pair
#   per principal named in a declarations file, `{ "<principal>": <uid>, ...
#   }`, mirroring `expected-cadence.json`'s own pattern (DC-2 precedent).
#   `--doctor-uid` is REMOVED, not aliased or deprecated-loud — the unit is
#   ours and was updated the same night; a compat shim nobody needs is drift
#   waiting.
#
#   The declarations file is a DEPLOYMENT DECLARATION, same standing as the
#   roster and expected-cadence.json: The arbiter writes it on the box, listing
#   each rostered depositor this deployment wants a journal pair for and its
#   real OS uid (`id <principal>`). R-V3 forbids guessing a principal's
#   identity, so no placeholder file is written here and none is checked in
#   with real uids: an invented or stale uid that happens to be wrong would
#   silently miscount, which is worse than the honest CANNOT_ASSESS this unit
#   reads without the flag. OWED to whoever writes that file — add
#   `--journal-declarations <path>` to ExecStart below once it exists. Until
#   then the whole pair family prints CANNOT_ASSESS, loud and named, every day.
#
# ── EXIT CODES, so an operator reading `systemctl status` knows what happened ─
#
#   0 written   1 could not start (a flag)   3 the store would not read
#   4 the report directory is not there      5 that day's report already exists
#   6 written, but the outbox would not list
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

[Unit]
Description=MICA C6/W5 — the daily witness report (a reader, not a monitor)
Documentation=file:///opt/mica-machine/src/report/mica-report.ts
RequiresMountsFor=/var/lib/mica/witness
RequiresMountsFor=/var/lib/mica/report

[Service]
Type=oneshot

# ── THE PRINCIPAL IS A DEPLOY DECLARATION — the arbiter states it, same as
#    --witness-set-id. It must be an account in `mica-readers` (recipe §3), and
#    it must NOT be `mica-witness`: a reader that is the writer proves nothing
#    about whether the book can be read by the roles entitled to it.
#    The placeholder is deliberately unusable-looking.
User=REPLACE-WITH-THE-REPORT-PRINCIPAL
Group=REPLACE-WITH-THE-REPORT-PRINCIPAL
# The read on the store (0640 mica-witness:mica-readers) and on the outbox
# (0750 mica-witness:mica-readers) comes from HERE and nowhere else.
SupplementaryGroups=mica-readers

WorkingDirectory=/opt/mica-machine
# The binary directly, never `npx` — §5.4 correction 4. An npx cache would be
# written into this principal's home.
ExecStart=/opt/mica-machine/node_modules/.bin/ts-node src/report/mica-report.ts \
  --store /var/lib/mica/witness/store.jsonl \
  --outbox /var/lib/mica/witness/outbox \
  --report-dir /var/lib/mica/report \
  --log /var/lib/mica/mica-log.jsonl \
  --cadence-file /var/lib/mica/report/expected-cadence.json \
  --clerk-stamps-file /var/lib/mica/clerk-clock-stamps.jsonl \
  --registrar-log /var/lib/mica/precommit/registrar-log-chained.jsonl \
  --journal-declarations /etc/mica/journal-declarations.json \
  --deployment-facts /etc/mica/deployment-facts.json

# 0027: the report is written 0640. Its GROUP comes from the report directory's
# SETGID bit (§5.5 asks for 2750, not 0750) — without setgid the file lands in
# this principal's primary group and no other reader can open it, in a directory
# they can enter. The same hazard sits on the outbox today; see the receipt.
UMask=0027

NoNewPrivileges=yes
ProtectSystem=strict
ProtectHome=yes
# ProtectSystem=strict makes everything read-only; the witness paths stay
# readable, and this is the ONE path this unit may write.
ReadWritePaths=/var/lib/mica/report
PrivateTmp=yes

# NO [Install] SECTION, DELIBERATELY. See the header.
